WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.919 --> 00:00:04.980
When you imagine a playground, chances are
it looks something like this.

00:00:04.980 --> 00:00:09.740
There’s usually a slide, a bridge, and a
high point with a domed roof.

00:00:09.740 --> 00:00:14.120
That’s what makes this a playground, and
this, and this.

00:00:14.120 --> 00:00:15.620
But what about this?

00:00:15.620 --> 00:00:17.120
This isn’t a junkyard.

00:00:17.120 --> 00:00:19.130
It’s called an adventure playground.

00:00:19.130 --> 00:00:24.020
Here, there are no plastic play structures
– just things like old tires, wood planks,

00:00:24.020 --> 00:00:25.300
hammers and nails.

00:00:25.300 --> 00:00:30.320
Places like this represent one of the most
debated ideas in play architecture: that playgrounds

00:00:30.320 --> 00:00:33.350
should be designed to let kids take more risks.

00:00:33.350 --> 00:00:38.469
Now, this nightmare for helicopter parents
is the hottest new thing in American playgrounds,

00:00:38.469 --> 00:00:43.210
because there’s growing evidence that play
like this is a whole lot healthier — and

00:00:43.210 --> 00:00:45.060
safer — for kids.

00:00:50.960 --> 00:00:53.160
They can play with very dangerous
tools,

00:00:53.160 --> 00:00:56.499
they can take really dangerous risks and overcome
them.

00:00:56.499 --> 00:01:01.440
And this fills up a tremendous sense of self
confidence in themselves, which is really

00:01:01.440 --> 00:01:03.399
quite fascinating to watch.

00:01:03.399 --> 00:01:04.820
That’s Marjory Allen.

00:01:04.820 --> 00:01:08.660
She was a British landscape architect and
children’s welfare advocate around the middle

00:01:08.660 --> 00:01:09.869
of the century.

00:01:09.869 --> 00:01:15.189
In 1945, she visited Copenhagen, where she
met an architect named Carl Theodor Sorensen.

00:01:15.189 --> 00:01:20.119
Two years earlier, during the German occupation
of Denmark, Sorensen noticed a problem: kids

00:01:20.120 --> 00:01:22.500
in his neighborhood weren’t using playgrounds.

00:01:22.500 --> 00:01:26.720
In fact, they were playing just about everywhere
else — even in construction sites and bombed

00:01:26.720 --> 00:01:27.859
out buildings.

00:01:27.859 --> 00:01:31.950
So in a housing development in the suburbs
of Copenhagen, Sorensen closed off an empty

00:01:31.950 --> 00:01:35.999
lot and filled it with building materials,
discarded objects, and tools.

00:01:35.999 --> 00:01:40.009
Here, kids could dig, build, and invent on
their own.

00:01:40.009 --> 00:01:45.150
The play structures were ultimately designed
by the kids themselves.

00:01:45.150 --> 00:01:49.850
Sorensen called it a junk playground — and
kids and parents loved it.

00:01:49.850 --> 00:01:54.300
When she returned to England, Marjory Allen
started opening similar playgrounds across London.

00:01:54.300 --> 00:01:57.600
And she renamed them: from junk to adventure.

00:01:57.609 --> 00:02:00.380
From there, they became a global phenomenon.

00:02:00.380 --> 00:02:05.060
They spread to Minneapolis, Boston, Toronto,
Tokyo, Houston, Berkeley, Berlin.

00:02:05.260 --> 00:02:10.400
And to create these playgrounds, designers
had to introduce a critical element: Controlled risk.

00:02:11.360 --> 00:02:14.960
In this context, a risk isn’t the same thing
as a hazard.

00:02:14.970 --> 00:02:19.569
When you’re climbing a tall tree, a rotten
branch is a hazard: the threat is unexpected.

00:02:19.569 --> 00:02:25.050
But how high you climb is a risk: it’s manageable,
and requires you to actively make a decision.

00:02:25.050 --> 00:02:30.060
You can break the elements of controlled risk
down into six categories: heights, speed,

00:02:30.060 --> 00:02:36.060
tools, dangerous elements, rough and tumble
play, and the ability to disappear, or become lost.

00:02:36.100 --> 00:02:39.240
And a good adventure playground includes a
mix of these.

00:02:39.240 --> 00:02:42.340
Designers also focus on separation of space.

00:02:42.349 --> 00:02:47.569
To give kids the feeling of discovering things
on their own, parents have to stay out.

00:02:47.569 --> 00:02:51.940
That can mean installing a physical barrier
— or providing things like restrooms, cafés,

00:02:51.940 --> 00:02:55.760
and seating, so that parental experience isn’t
an afterthought.

00:02:55.769 --> 00:02:58.480
Finally, designers fill it with loose parts.

00:02:58.480 --> 00:03:03.040
These are the manipulatable objects — the
planks, barrels, bricks, and tools — that

00:03:03.040 --> 00:03:04.240
fuel risky play.

00:03:05.650 --> 00:03:09.600
The idea behind all these design elements
is that kids respond well to being treated

00:03:09.610 --> 00:03:14.360
seriously: if they’re presented with risky
items with a serious functional purpose, they’ll

00:03:14.360 --> 00:03:17.180
respond cautiously and conduct more experimentation.

00:03:17.180 --> 00:03:21.360
But if presented with an overly safe, static
space, they often wind up seeking dangerous

00:03:21.360 --> 00:03:25.819
thrills that the built environment fails to
provide, which can result in higher injury

00:03:25.819 --> 00:03:28.170
rates than risky play at adventure playgrounds.

00:03:28.170 --> 00:03:33.060
In the US, a culture of lawsuit-proof playscape
design means that overly safe playgrounds

00:03:33.060 --> 00:03:34.260
are the norm.

00:03:34.260 --> 00:03:38.209
And design philosophy has focused on how to
reduce height, movement, and hard materials.

00:03:38.209 --> 00:03:40.550
That hasn’t made playgrounds better.

00:03:40.550 --> 00:03:45.560
When Marjory Allen visited American playgrounds
in 1965, she called them “an administrator’s

00:03:45.560 --> 00:03:47.790
heaven and a child’s hell.”

00:03:47.790 --> 00:03:51.720
But adventure playgrounds have recently begun
to catch on in the US — perhaps due to an

00:03:51.720 --> 00:03:54.299
effort to introduce more unstructured play.

00:03:54.720 --> 00:03:57.740
And their construction comes with a fair share
of criticism.

00:03:57.750 --> 00:04:02.080
“They’re making kids play with hammers
and nails — that’s not adventure, it's

00:04:02.080 --> 00:04:03.080
just work.

00:04:03.080 --> 00:04:06.620
They’re tricking kids into building their
own playground.

00:04:06.620 --> 00:04:11.500
Adventure playgrounds do have downsides: They’re
pretty ugly, they require a lot of space,

00:04:11.510 --> 00:04:13.750
and they need resources to staff and maintain.

00:04:13.750 --> 00:04:16.840
And as with any playground, there is opportunity
for injury.

00:04:16.840 --> 00:04:21.590
But the underlying philosophy of risky play
can help kids live better lives.

00:04:21.590 --> 00:04:24.600
For one thing, riskier playgrounds encourage
more activity.

00:04:24.600 --> 00:04:29.440
A study comparing playgrounds in London, where
risky play spaces are popular, to those in

00:04:29.440 --> 00:04:33.670
San Francisco, Los Angeles, and New York found
that children using London’s playgrounds

00:04:33.670 --> 00:04:36.510
were up to 18 percent more physically active.

00:04:36.510 --> 00:04:40.070
The London playgrounds were cheaper and boasted
fewer injuries, too.

00:04:40.070 --> 00:04:43.630
And multiple studies have shown that children
who engage in risky play have better risk

00:04:43.630 --> 00:04:46.460
detection, creativity, and self esteem.

00:04:47.800 --> 00:04:52.480
The playground is one of the only kinds of
architecture designed specifically for children.

00:04:52.480 --> 00:04:57.600
And if the standard model we’ve decided
on is seen as boring by its users — that’s

00:04:57.600 --> 00:04:58.580
a problem.

00:04:58.580 --> 00:05:01.920
Better design can fix that — even if it’s
a little risky.

00:05:04.560 --> 00:05:07.240
I had to look through so many pictures of playgrounds for this story

00:05:07.240 --> 00:05:09.840
that I decided to use Wix to create a website

00:05:09.840 --> 00:05:15.180
collecting all of the ugliest and saddest pictures of playgrounds that I could find.

00:05:15.220 --> 00:05:20.940
And now I have a perfectly curated arrangement of pictures of playgrounds next to graveyards

00:05:20.940 --> 00:05:27.440
and slides leading into dumpsters and whatever these kid-friendly statues are. If

00:05:27.440 --> 00:05:30.780
